<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shishunaga dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 413–345 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shishunaga dynasty ruled the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from around the late 5th century BCE until about 345 BCE. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga">Shishunaga</a>, who conquered Avanti, it kept its court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>. In the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalashoka">Kalashoka</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-buddhist-council">Second Buddhist Council</a> is said to have met at Vaishali. The last Shishunaga ruler was overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda">Mahapadma Nanda</a>, founder of the Nanda Empire.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Shaishunaga dynasty was possibly the second ruling dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>. According to the Buddhist text <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a> it followed Nagadashaka of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryanka-dynasty">Haryanka dynasty</a>. The Brahminic Puranas give a different list of kings with a different chronology, and Jain texts do not mention the dynasty at all. They say instead that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayin">Udayin</a> of the Haryanka dynasty was killed by an assassin from a rival kingdom and, being childless, was succeeded by Nanda, whom his ministers chose. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga">Shishunaga</a>, the founder, had been an amatya, or minister, of the last Haryanka ruler, Nāgadāsaka, and came to the throne after a popular rebellion in c. 413 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga">Shishunaga</a> founded his dynasty in 413 BCE with a capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajgir</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. Buddhist sources say he had a secondary capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a>, formerly the capital of Vajji, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> conquered it. He ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pradyota-dynasty">Pradyota dynasty</a> of Avanti, which ended the centuries-old rivalry between the two kingdoms, and annexed Avanti into Magadha.</p><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga">Shishunaga</a> was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalashoka">Kakavarna</a>, and according to the Sinhala chronicles by Kalashoka; some historians hold that the two are the same. The Second Buddhist council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> and the final transfer of the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> are the two main events of his reign. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harshacharita">Harshacharita</a> he was killed by a dagger thrust into his throat. Buddhist tradition says ten sons of Kalashoka ruled simultaneously, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a> lists seven kings after Kākavarṇa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> count Nandivardhana as the ninth Shaishunaga king and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahanandin">Mahanandin</a> as the tenth and last. Mahanandin was killed by Mahapadma, his illegitimate son, and the Puranas say the Nanda Empire, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda">Mahapadma Nanda</a>, followed the Shaishunagas in c. 345 BCE. The Buddhist tradition says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalashoka">Kalashoka</a>'s sons were ousted by Ugrasena Nanda. The historian K. T. S. Sarao, who favours the Buddhist short chronology, dates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayin">Udayin</a>'s reign to c. 373–357 BCE and suggests that the lists of successors may describe rulers in different places at the same time while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> was divided. Keay, another supporter of the short chronology, likewise says the succession in this period is very uncertain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shaishunaga dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Shishunaga dynasty</b> completed the expansion by conquering Avanti in western India, leaving Magadha the dominant power of northern India by the mid-fourth century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 345 BCE Mahapadma Nanda, said by tradition to be of low birth, overthrew the last <b>Shishunaga</b> ruler of the Kingdom of Magadha.</blockquote><a href="/state/nanda-empire">Nanda Empire</a></li></ul></section>
